</section>
<section id="Pt2" class="ltx_part">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_part">Appendix</h2>

<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Rotary Position Embeddings from a Group Theoretical Perspective</h3>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A fundamental challenge in geometric deep learning is creating position representations that respect underlying symmetries. For data in <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, our goal is to define a high-dimensional position embedding, <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{E}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐄</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{E}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is equivariant to translations. This requires that for any translation vector <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, the embedding transforms predictably: <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{E}(\mathbf{p}_{0}+\mathbf{p})=\bm{\rho}(\mathbf{p})\mathbf{E}(\mathbf{p}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐄</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐄</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{E}(\mathbf{p}_{0}+\mathbf{p})=\bm{\rho}(\mathbf{p})\mathbf{E}(\mathbf{p}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}(\mathbf{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}(\mathbf{p})</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear transformation. Group representation theory provides the formal tools to construct such embeddings.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>The Theoretical Toolkit</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To proceed, we first define the essential concepts required for our construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Representation)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A linear representation of a group <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> on a vector space <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a group homomorphism <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\mathcal{G}\to\text{GL}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">GL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\mathcal{G}\to\text{GL}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{GL}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">GL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{GL}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is the general linear group of invertible linear transformations on <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure that the positional encoding does not arbitrarily amplify or diminish feature magnitudes, which would destabilize learning, we require the representations to be length-preserving. This leads to the concept of a unitary representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Unitary Representation)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A representation <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unitary</span> if it maps group elements to unitary operators, i.e., <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\mathcal{G}\to\text{U}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">U</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\mathcal{G}\to\text{U}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. For real-valued representations, this corresponds to orthogonality, <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(g)^{-1}=\rho(g)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(g)^{-1}=\rho(g)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Just as a complex signal can be decomposed into pure frequencies, a general representation can be broken down into fundamental building blocks known as irreducible representations (irreps).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Irreducible Representation)<span id="Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An <span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">irreducible representation</span> (irrep) is a representation acting on a vector space <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> that has no non-trivial invariant subspaces.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Constructing the RoPE Operator</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With these formal tools, we can now build the RoPE operator. The irreps of the translation group <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{R}^{d},+)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{R}^{d},+)</annotation></semantics></math> are indexed by a frequency vector <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\omega}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\omega}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and are given by one-dimensional, unitary representations:</p>
<table id="A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\bm{\omega}}(\mathbf{p})=e^{i\bm{\omega}^{\top}\mathbf{p}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝎</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\bm{\omega}}(\mathbf{p})=e^{i\bm{\omega}^{\top}\mathbf{p}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This exponential form is the unique continuous solution to the group’s homomorphism property, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\mathbf{p}_{1}+\mathbf{p}_{2})=\rho(\mathbf{p}_{1})\rho(\mathbf{p}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\mathbf{p}_{1}+\mathbf{p}_{2})=\rho(\mathbf{p}_{1})\rho(\mathbf{p}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the imaginary exponent ensures unitarity.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, neural networks typically operate on real numbers. We can obtain a real-valued irrep by combining pairs of conjugate frequencies, <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\omega}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\omega}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\bm{\omega}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\bm{\omega}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields a 2D irreducible representation that takes the familiar form of a rotation matrix:</p>
<table id="A1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\bm{\omega}_{k}}(\mathbf{p})=\begin{pmatrix}\cos(\bm{\omega}_{k}^{\top}\mathbf{p})&amp;-\sin(\bm{\omega}_{k}^{\top}\mathbf{p})\\
\sin(\bm{\omega}_{k}^{\top}\mathbf{p})&amp;\cos(\bm{\omega}_{k}^{\top}\mathbf{p})\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\bm{\omega}_{k}}(\mathbf{p})=\begin{pmatrix}\cos(\bm{\omega}_{k}^{\top}\mathbf{p})&amp;-\sin(\bm{\omega}_{k}^{\top}\mathbf{p})\\
\sin(\bm{\omega}_{k}^{\top}\mathbf{p})&amp;\cos(\bm{\omega}_{k}^{\top}\mathbf{p})\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">To create a high-dimensional embedding, we simply select a set of frequencies <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega=\{\bm{\omega}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{d^{\prime}/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=\{\bm{\omega}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{d^{\prime}/2}</annotation></semantics></math> and stack these 2D rotation blocks along the diagonal of a larger matrix. This results in a single, block-diagonal transformation that correctly and equivariantly updates the entire embedding for a given translation <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\mathbf{p})=\text{diag}(\rho^{\bm{\omega}_{1}}(\mathbf{p}),\dots,\rho_{\bm{\omega}_{d^{\prime}/2}}(\mathbf{p}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝆</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\mathbf{p})=\text{diag}(\rho^{\bm{\omega}_{1}}(\mathbf{p}),\dots,\rho_{\bm{\omega}_{d^{\prime}/2}}(\mathbf{p})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">This is the core mechanism behind Rotary Position Embeddings. Its structure guarantees both equivariance and computational efficiency, as each 2D component can be rotated independently.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Rotary Position Embedding (RoPE) Operator)<span id="Thmdefinition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The RoPE operator <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\mathbf{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛒</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\mathbf{p})</annotation></semantics></math> for a position <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the block-diagonal rotation matrix defined above (Equation <a href="#A1.E15" title="In A.2 Constructing the RoPE Operator ‣ Appendix A Rotary Position Embeddings from a Group Theoretical Perspective ‣ Appendix ‣ Platonic Transformers: A Solid Choice For Equivariance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), constructed from a set of frequencies <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>. The application of RoPE to a feature vector <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the matrix-vector product: <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\mathbf{p})\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛒</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\mathbf{p})\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. For this operation to be well-defined, the feature dimension <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> must be even.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Translation Invariance in Attention</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the RoPE operator provides an <span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">equivariant</span> transformation for feature vectors, its crucial benefit within the Transformer architecture is that it makes the attention score <span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">invariant</span> to global translations. This property ensures that the attention mechanism only considers the relative positions of tokens, which is the inductive bias we seek. We formalize this key result below.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmproposition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Translation Invariance of the RoPE Attention Score)<span id="Thmproposition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The attention score computed using RoPE, <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{attn}(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{k},\Delta\mathbf{p})=\mathbf{q}^{\top}\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\Delta\mathbf{p})\mathbf{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>attn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝛒</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐤</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{attn}(\mathbf{q},\mathbf{k},\Delta\mathbf{p})=\mathbf{q}^{\top}\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\Delta\mathbf{p})\mathbf{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{p}=\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{p}=\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, is invariant to a global translation of the coordinate system.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let the positions <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> be translated by an arbitrary vector <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in new positions <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{i}=\mathbf{p}_{i}+\mathbf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐭</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{i}=\mathbf{p}_{i}+\mathbf{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{j}=\mathbf{p}_{j}+\mathbf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐭</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{j}=\mathbf{p}_{j}+\mathbf{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The new relative displacement vector, <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{p}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{p}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, is:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{p}^{\prime}=\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{j}-\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{i}=(\mathbf{p}_{j}+\mathbf{t})-(\mathbf{p}_{i}+\mathbf{t})=\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i}=\Delta\mathbf{p}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐭</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐭</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{p}^{\prime}=\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{j}-\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{i}=(\mathbf{p}_{j}+\mathbf{t})-(\mathbf{p}_{i}+\mathbf{t})=\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i}=\Delta\mathbf{p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since the relative displacement vector is unchanged by the global translation, the RoPE operator applied to it also remains unchanged: <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\Delta\mathbf{p}^{\prime})=\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\Delta\mathbf{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝆</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝆</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\Delta\mathbf{p}^{\prime})=\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\Delta\mathbf{p})</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the attention score, which depends only on the content vectors and this operator, is invariant to the translation:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}^{\top}\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\Delta\mathbf{p}^{\prime})\mathbf{k}=\mathbf{q}^{\top}\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\Delta\mathbf{p})\mathbf{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝆</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐤</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝆</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐤</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}^{\top}\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\Delta\mathbf{p}^{\prime})\mathbf{k}=\mathbf{q}^{\top}\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\Delta\mathbf{p})\mathbf{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">This formally demonstrates that RoPE imparts translation invariance to the attention mechanism.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>A Fourier Perspective</h4>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The principle of constructing equivariant functions from irreducible representations is deeply connected to Fourier analysis. The Fourier transform provides a way to decompose any function on a group into a weighted sum (or integral) over its irreps. For the translation group on <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, these irreps are precisely the complex exponentials we used as our building blocks. Therefore, RoPE can be understood as a practical application of Fourier theory, using a discrete basis of Fourier modes (the chosen frequencies <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>) to represent the positional signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Fourier Transform on <math id="Thmdefinition5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmdefinition5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The forward Fourier transform <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}:L^{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})\to L^{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:L^{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})\to L^{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> maps a function <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to its frequency-space representation <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. The coefficient for a frequency <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection of <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> onto the corresponding irrep <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\bm{\omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝛚</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\bm{\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}(\bm{\omega})=\mathcal{F}\{f\}(\bm{\omega})=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(\mathbf{p})\overline{\rho_{\bm{\omega}}(\mathbf{p})}\,d\mathbf{p}=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(\mathbf{p})e^{-i\bm{\omega}^{\top}\mathbf{p}}\,d\mathbf{p}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝎</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}(\bm{\omega})=\mathcal{F}\{f\}(\bm{\omega})=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(\mathbf{p})\overline{\rho_{\bm{\omega}}(\mathbf{p})}\,d\mathbf{p}=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(\mathbf{p})e^{-i\bm{\omega}^{\top}\mathbf{p}}\,d\mathbf{p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The inverse transform reconstructs the function by integrating over all irreps:</span></p>
<table id="A1.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{p})=\mathcal{F}^{-1}\{\hat{f}\}(\mathbf{p})=\frac{1}{(2\pi)^{d}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\hat{f}(\bm{\omega})\rho_{\bm{\omega}}(\mathbf{p})\,d\bm{\omega}=\frac{1}{(2\pi)^{d}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\hat{f}(\bm{\omega})e^{i\bm{\omega}^{\top}\mathbf{p}}\,d\bm{\omega}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝎</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝝎</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝝎</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{p})=\mathcal{F}^{-1}\{\hat{f}\}(\mathbf{p})=\frac{1}{(2\pi)^{d}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\hat{f}(\bm{\omega})\rho_{\bm{\omega}}(\mathbf{p})\,d\bm{\omega}=\frac{1}{(2\pi)^{d}}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\hat{f}(\bm{\omega})e^{i\bm{\omega}^{\top}\mathbf{p}}\,d\bm{\omega}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Related Works</h3>

<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Equivariant Transformers.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SE(3) Transformer, EFA, G-Hyena, EScAIP, GATr</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Approximate Equivariance.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Frame Averaging, REMUL, EquiGrad in Orb-v3, SymPE</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Equivariance Properties of Platonic Transformers</h3>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formally establish the equivariance of our proposed architecture. We consider a point cloud <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{p}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i},s_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{p}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i},s_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of positions, vectors, and scalars. A global rotation <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on these inputs as <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{i}\mapsto R\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{i}\mapsto R\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{i}\mapsto R\mathbf{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{i}\mapsto R\mathbf{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}\mapsto s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}\mapsto s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Equivariant Feature Lifting.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Input features are first lifted to functions on the group <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. The lifting operator, <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Lift}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Lift</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Lift}</annotation></semantics></math>, maps the input point cloud to a set of feature maps <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{f}_{i}:\mathcal{G}\to\mathbb{R}^{C}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{f}_{i}:\mathcal{G}\to\mathbb{R}^{C}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Scalar components are copied to each frame, while vector components (from <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{i},\mathbf{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) are lifted by projecting them onto each reference frame. This projection means expressing the vector’s coordinates in the local basis of a given frame <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{R}\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{R}\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is achieved by the transformation <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{R}^{-1}\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{R}^{-1}\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. This lifting procedure is equivariant by construction: a global rotation <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> of the input point cloud results in the lifted feature maps transforming via the left regular representation, <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. That is:</p>
<table id="A3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\operatorname{Lift}(R\cdot\text{cloud}))_{i}(\tilde{R})=(\operatorname{Lift}(\text{cloud}))_{i}(R^{-1}\tilde{R})\triangleq(L_{R}\mathbf{f}_{i})(\tilde{R})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Lift</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>cloud</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Lift</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>cloud</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\operatorname{Lift}(R\cdot\text{cloud}))_{i}(\tilde{R})=(\operatorname{Lift}(\text{cloud}))_{i}(R^{-1}\tilde{R})\triangleq(L_{R}\mathbf{f}_{i})(\tilde{R}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Equivariant Linear Layers.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All linear layers <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> in our network are implemented as point-wise group convolutions, as shown in Eq. <a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.2 Equivariant Linear Layers and Fixed Computation Graph ‣ 3 The Platonic Transformer ‣ Platonic Transformers: A Solid Choice For Equivariance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. These layers are equivariant to the action of the group by construction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al., 2019</a>, Thm. 3.1)</cite>, satisfying <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(L_{R}\mathbf{f}_{i})=L_{R}(\Phi(\mathbf{f}_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(L_{R}\mathbf{f}_{i})=L_{R}(\Phi(\mathbf{f}_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This leads to our main proposition regarding the attention mechanism.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4</span></span><span id="Thmproposition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Equivariant Attention)<span id="Thmproposition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the queries <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, keys <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and values <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be equivariant feature maps produced by the equivariant linear layers. The RoPE-enhanced attention mechanism (Eq. <a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.1 Weight-sharing across RoPE Embeddings ‣ 3 The Platonic Transformer ‣ Platonic Transformers: A Solid Choice For Equivariance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), which computes outputs <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, is an equivariant operation. That is, if the inputs transform as <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{i}\mapsto L_{R}\mathbf{f}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{i}\mapsto L_{R}\mathbf{f}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the outputs transform as <math id="Thmproposition4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{i}\mapsto L_{R}\mathbf{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{i}\mapsto L_{R}\mathbf{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let the inputs to the attention layer (<math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i},K_{i},V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i},K_{i},V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) transform under a global rotation <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}_{i}=L_{R}Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}_{i}=L_{R}Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\prime}_{i}=L_{R}K_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\prime}_{i}=L_{R}K_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}_{i}=L_{R}V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}_{i}=L_{R}V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We analyze the transformation of each component of the attention calculation.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The score function <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{ij}(\tilde{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{ij}(\tilde{R})</annotation></semantics></math>, which depends on <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}(\tilde{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}(\tilde{R})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{j}(\tilde{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{j}(\tilde{R})</annotation></semantics></math> (and potentially RoPE terms derived from lifted positions), will transform as:</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s^{\prime}_{ij}(\tilde{R})=\text{score}(Q^{\prime}_{i}(\tilde{R}),K^{\prime}_{i}(\tilde{R}),\dots)=\text{score}(Q_{i}(R^{-1}\tilde{R}),K_{j}(R^{-1}\tilde{R}),\dots)=s_{ij}(R^{-1}\tilde{R})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>score</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>score</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s^{\prime}_{ij}(\tilde{R})=\text{score}(Q^{\prime}_{i}(\tilde{R}),K^{\prime}_{i}(\tilde{R}),\dots)=\text{score}(Q_{i}(R^{-1}\tilde{R}),K_{j}(R^{-1}\tilde{R}),\dots)=s_{ij}(R^{-1}\tilde{R}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">This means the score function itself is equivariant, <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}_{ij}=L_{R}s_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}_{ij}=L_{R}s_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the softmax operator is applied point-wise for each frame <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{R}</annotation></semantics></math> over the index <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, the attention weights also transform equivariantly:</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{attn}^{\prime}_{ij}(\tilde{R})=\underset{j}{\operatorname{softmax}}(s^{\prime}_{ij}(\tilde{R}))=\underset{j}{\operatorname{softmax}}(s_{ij}(R^{-1}\tilde{R}))=\operatorname{attn}_{ij}(R^{-1}\tilde{R})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>attn</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>softmax</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑗</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>softmax</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑗</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>attn</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{attn}^{\prime}_{ij}(\tilde{R})=\underset{j}{\operatorname{softmax}}(s^{\prime}_{ij}(\tilde{R}))=\underset{j}{\operatorname{softmax}}(s_{ij}(R^{-1}\tilde{R}))=\operatorname{attn}_{ij}(R^{-1}\tilde{R}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, the output feature map <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> transforms as:</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{y}^{\prime}_{i}(\tilde{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{y}^{\prime}_{i}(\tilde{R})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\operatorname{attn}^{\prime}_{ij}(\tilde{R})V^{\prime}_{j}(\tilde{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>attn</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\operatorname{attn}^{\prime}_{ij}(\tilde{R})V^{\prime}_{j}(\tilde{R})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\operatorname{attn}_{ij}(R^{-1}\tilde{R})V_{j}(R^{-1}\tilde{R})=\mathbf{y}_{i}(R^{-1}\tilde{R})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>attn</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\operatorname{attn}_{ij}(R^{-1}\tilde{R})V_{j}(R^{-1}\tilde{R})=\mathbf{y}_{i}(R^{-1}\tilde{R}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, the output transforms as <math id="A3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}^{\prime}_{i}=L_{R}\mathbf{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}^{\prime}_{i}=L_{R}\mathbf{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, proving the attention mechanism is equivariant.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Proofs</h3>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 2 (Linear RoPE Attention as Dynamic Convolution). ‣ 4.1 Platonic Transformer as Dynamic Group Convolution ‣ 4 Inductive Bias of Platonic Transformers ‣ Platonic Transformers: A Solid Choice For Equivariance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></h4>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We seek to show that the unnormalized attention score, which defines the kernel <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\mathbf{q}_{i}}(\Delta\mathbf{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\mathbf{q}_{i}}(\Delta\mathbf{p})</annotation></semantics></math>, takes the form of a sparse Fourier series whose coefficients are linear projections of the query <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let the relative position be <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{p}=\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{p}=\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. With a constant key vector <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{j}=\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{j}=\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the kernel is defined by the attention score:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\mathbf{q}_{i}}(\Delta\mathbf{p})=(\bm{\rho}(\mathbf{p}_{i})\mathbf{q}_{i})^{\top}(\bm{\rho}(\mathbf{p}_{j})\mathbf{1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\mathbf{q}_{i}}(\Delta\mathbf{p})=(\bm{\rho}(\mathbf{p}_{i})\mathbf{q}_{i})^{\top}(\bm{\rho}(\mathbf{p}_{j})\mathbf{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Using the properties of the RoPE operator <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>, this simplifies to:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\mathbf{q}_{i}}(\Delta\mathbf{p})=\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\bm{\rho}(\mathbf{p}_{i})^{\top}\bm{\rho}(\mathbf{p}_{j})\mathbf{1}=\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\bm{\rho}(\Delta\mathbf{p})\mathbf{1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\mathbf{q}_{i}}(\Delta\mathbf{p})=\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\bm{\rho}(\mathbf{p}_{i})^{\top}\bm{\rho}(\mathbf{p}_{j})\mathbf{1}=\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\bm{\rho}(\Delta\mathbf{p})\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The RoPE matrix <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\rho}(\Delta\mathbf{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\rho}(\Delta\mathbf{p})</annotation></semantics></math> is block-diagonal, consisting of <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d/2</annotation></semantics></math> independent 2D rotation blocks. We can therefore analyze the contribution from a single block <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and sum the results. Let <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{k}=\bm{\omega}_{k}^{\top}\Delta\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{k}=\bm{\omega}_{k}^{\top}\Delta\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. The contribution from block <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}=\begin{pmatrix}q_{2k-1}&amp;q_{2k}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\cos(\theta_{k})&amp;-\sin(\theta_{k})\\
\sin(\theta_{k})&amp;\cos(\theta_{k})\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1\\
1\end{pmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}=\begin{pmatrix}q_{2k-1}&amp;q_{2k}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\cos(\theta_{k})&amp;-\sin(\theta_{k})\\
\sin(\theta_{k})&amp;\cos(\theta_{k})\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1\\
1\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Performing the matrix-vector multiplications, we get:</p>
<table id="A4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}q_{2k-1}&amp;q_{2k}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\cos(\theta_{k})-\sin(\theta_{k})\\
\sin(\theta_{k})+\cos(\theta_{k})\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{pmatrix}q_{2k-1}&amp;q_{2k}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\cos(\theta_{k})-\sin(\theta_{k})\\
\sin(\theta_{k})+\cos(\theta_{k})\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=q_{2k-1}(\cos(\theta_{k})-\sin(\theta_{k}))+q_{2k}(\sin(\theta_{k})+\cos(\theta_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=q_{2k-1}(\cos(\theta_{k})-\sin(\theta_{k}))+q_{2k}(\sin(\theta_{k})+\cos(\theta_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Grouping terms by <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos(\theta_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos(\theta_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sin(\theta_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sin(\theta_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> reveals the linear projections:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}=\underbrace{(q_{2k-1}+q_{2k})}_{a_{k}(\mathbf{q}_{i})}\cos(\theta_{k})+\underbrace{(q_{2k}-q_{2k-1})}_{b_{k}(\mathbf{q}_{i})}\sin(\theta_{k})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}=\underbrace{(q_{2k-1}+q_{2k})}_{a_{k}(\mathbf{q}_{i})}\cos(\theta_{k})+\underbrace{(q_{2k}-q_{2k-1})}_{b_{k}(\mathbf{q}_{i})}\sin(\theta_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">The Fourier coefficients <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}(\mathbf{q}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}(\mathbf{q}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k}(\mathbf{q}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k}(\mathbf{q}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are thus simple linear combinations of the query vector’s elements. Summing over all <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,d/2</annotation></semantics></math> yields the complete kernel <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\mathbf{q}_{i}}(\Delta\mathbf{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\mathbf{q}_{i}}(\Delta\mathbf{p})</annotation></semantics></math>, which has the exact form stated in the proposition.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Proof of Corollary <a href="#Thmcorollary1" title="Corollary 1 (Linear-Time Complexity). ‣ 4.1 Platonic Transformer as Dynamic Group Convolution ‣ 4 Inductive Bias of Platonic Transformers ‣ Platonic Transformers: A Solid Choice For Equivariance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The linear-time complexity is achieved by expressing the operation in matrix form and re-ordering the computation. Let <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}^{\prime}_{i}=\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\mathbf{p}_{i})\mathbf{q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝆</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}^{\prime}_{i}=\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\mathbf{p}_{i})\mathbf{q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}^{\prime}_{j}=\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\mathbf{p}_{j})\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝆</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}^{\prime}_{j}=\bm{\rho}_{\Omega}(\mathbf{p}_{j})\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the matrix with rows <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{q}^{\prime}_{i})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{q}^{\prime}_{i})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the matrix with rows <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{k}^{\prime}_{j})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{k}^{\prime}_{j})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the matrix of value vectors. The output matrix <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}=(\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}(\mathbf{K}^{\prime})^{\top})\mathbf{V}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}=(\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}(\mathbf{K}^{\prime})^{\top})\mathbf{V}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">By the associativity of matrix multiplication, this can be computed as <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}((\mathbf{K}^{\prime})^{\top}\mathbf{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}((\mathbf{K}^{\prime})^{\top}\mathbf{V})</annotation></semantics></math>. The term <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{K}^{\prime})^{\top}\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{K}^{\prime})^{\top}\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math> costs <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(Nd^{\prime}d_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(Nd^{\prime}d_{v})</annotation></semantics></math> to compute, resulting in a <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}\times d_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}\times d_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> matrix. Multiplying this by <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> costs an additional <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(Nd^{\prime}d_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(Nd^{\prime}d_{v})</annotation></semantics></math>. The total complexity is therefore <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(Nd^{\prime}d_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(Nd^{\prime}d_{v})</annotation></semantics></math>, linear in the sequence length <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Proof of Platonic Transformers Implementing Group Convolutions</h4>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The dynamic convolution from Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 2 (Linear RoPE Attention as Dynamic Convolution). ‣ 4.1 Platonic Transformer as Dynamic Group Convolution ‣ 4 Inductive Bias of Platonic Transformers ‣ Platonic Transformers: A Solid Choice For Equivariance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> becomes a dynamic <span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">group convolution</span> within the Platonic Transformer. This is a direct consequence of applying the operation to lifted coordinates <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{i}(R)=R^{-1}\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{i}(R)=R^{-1}\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each reference frame <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the relative position vector becomes <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{-1}(\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{-1}(\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, the kernel’s input is transformed accordingly. The resulting output for each frame takes the form of a group cross-correlation<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
              Following common convention, we refer to this operation as a group convolution, though it is technically a cross-correlation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen &amp; Welling (2016)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bekkers (2020)</a></cite>.</span></span></span>:</p>
<table id="A4.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{i}(R)=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\phi_{\mathbf{q}_{i}(R)}\left(R^{-1}(\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i})\right)\mathbf{v}_{j}(R)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{i}(R)=\sum_{j=1}^{N}\phi_{\mathbf{q}_{i}(R)}\left(R^{-1}(\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i})\right)\mathbf{v}_{j}(R).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, the kernel <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\mathbf{q}_{i}(R)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\mathbf{q}_{i}(R)}</annotation></semantics></math> is steered by the group element <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, defining an equivariant dynamic group convolution.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Equivalent Attention via RoPE Base Frequency Steering</h3>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We achieve full equivariance to Euclidean transformations by making the RoPE operator dependent on a local reference frame <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> by projecting positions <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain positions <math id="A5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{i}(R):=R^{-1}\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{i}(R):=R^{-1}\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The attention scores <math id="A5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{ij}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{ij}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> for a query <math id="A5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}(R)_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}(R)_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and key <math id="A5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}(R)_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}(R)_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are computed as:</p>
<table id="A5.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s_{ij}(R)=\mathbf{q}_{i}(R)^{\top}\rho_{\Omega}((\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i})(R))\mathbf{k}_{j}(R)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s_{ij}(R)=\mathbf{q}_{i}(R)^{\top}\rho_{\Omega}((\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i})(R))\mathbf{k}_{j}(R),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">An equivalent approach is to steer the set of base RoPE frequencies <math id="A5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> for each frame, creating a frame-specific set <math id="A5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{R}=\{R\bm{\omega}_{k}\mid\bm{\omega}_{k}\in\Omega\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{R}=\{R\bm{\omega}_{k}\mid\bm{\omega}_{k}\in\Omega\}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reddy &amp; Chatterji, 1996</a>)</cite>. The attention scores are then computed as:</p>
<table id="A5.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{ij}(R)=\mathbf{q}_{i}(R)^{\top}\bm{\rho}_{\Omega_{R}}(\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i})\mathbf{k}_{j}(R)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝆</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{ij}(R)=\mathbf{q}_{i}(R)^{\top}\bm{\rho}_{\Omega_{R}}(\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i})\mathbf{k}_{j}(R).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="A5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{s}_{ij}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{s}_{ij}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{ij}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{ij}(R)</annotation></semantics></math> to be equivalent, we require that <math id="A5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\Omega}((\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i})(R))=\bm{\rho}_{\Omega_{R}}(\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝆</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\Omega}((\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i})(R))=\bm{\rho}_{\Omega_{R}}(\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For this, we need to show that <math id="A5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\omega}^{\top}_{k}\Delta\mathbf{p}(R)=(R\bm{\omega})^{\top}_{k}\Delta\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝝎</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\omega}^{\top}_{k}\Delta\mathbf{p}(R)=(R\bm{\omega})^{\top}_{k}\Delta\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{p}=\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{p}=\mathbf{p}_{j}-\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be the orthogonal matrix corresponding to <math id="A5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have:</span></p>
<table id="A5.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{\omega}^{\top}_{k}\Delta\mathbf{p}(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{\omega}^{\top}_{k}\Delta\mathbf{p}(R)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bm{\omega}^{\top}_{k}R^{-1}\Delta\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bm{\omega}^{\top}_{k}R^{-1}\Delta\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bm{\omega}^{\top}_{k}\mathbf{R}^{\top}\Delta\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bm{\omega}^{\top}_{k}\mathbf{R}^{\top}\Delta\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(\mathbf{R}\bm{\omega}_{k})^{\top}\Delta\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(\mathbf{R}\bm{\omega}_{k})^{\top}\Delta\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(R\bm{\omega}_{k})^{\top}\Delta\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(R\bm{\omega}_{k})^{\top}\Delta\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, projecting global positions or steering the base frequencies are equivalent.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By projecting the global positions, the RoPE attention mechanism remains identical to its traditional formulation. Steering the base frequencies, however, is often more computationally efficient, since the number of base frequencies is typically much smaller than the input sequence length.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Frame Averaging</h3>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Frame Averaging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puny et al., 2022</a>)</cite> (FA) imbues symmetry awareness to arbitrary neural networks. A backbone model <math id="A6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:V\rightarrow W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:V\rightarrow W</annotation></semantics></math> on normed spaces can be made equivariant (or invariant) to a group <math id="A6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, for two group representations <math id="A6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1}(g)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{2}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{2}(g)</annotation></semantics></math> that <math id="A6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> induces on <math id="A6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\rho_{1}(g)\cdot\mathbf{x})=\rho_{2}(g)\cdot\Phi(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\rho_{1}(g)\cdot\mathbf{x})=\rho_{2}(g)\cdot\Phi(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math> is the group action on elements from <math id="A6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">FA first constructs a <span id="A6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">frame</span> <math id="A6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x}):V\rightarrow 2^{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x}):V\rightarrow 2^{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the following properties:</p>
<ul id="A6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A frame is <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant if <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\rho_{1}(g)\mathbf{x})=g\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\rho_{1}(g)\mathbf{x})=g\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A6.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})=\{gh|h\in\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})=\{gh|h\in\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A frame is bounded over a domain <math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subset V</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the operator norm <math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\rho_{2}(g)\|_{\text{op}}\leq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mtext>op</mtext></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\rho_{2}(g)\|_{\text{op}}\leq c</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A6.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall g\in\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x}),\forall\mathbf{x}\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall g\in\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x}),\forall\mathbf{x}\in K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">To make the backbone <math id="A6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant, <span id="A6.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symmetrization</span> is applied on <math id="A6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> via the group averaging operator:</p>
<table id="A6.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A6.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\langle\Phi\rangle_{\mathcal{F}}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\langle\Phi\rangle_{\mathcal{F}}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})|}\sum_{g\in\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})}\rho_{2}(g)\Phi(\rho_{1}(g)^{-1}(\mathbf{x}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})|}\sum_{g\in\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})}\rho_{2}(g)\Phi(\rho_{1}(g)^{-1}(\mathbf{x})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">For equivariance to 3-dimensional Euclidean rigid-body transformations (translations, rotations, reflections), i.e., <math id="A6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}:=E(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}:=E(3)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed using PCA on <math id="A6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>; this involves computing the centroid <math id="A6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{t}=\frac{1}{n}\mathbf{x}^{\top}\mathbf{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{t}=\frac{1}{n}\mathbf{x}^{\top}\mathbf{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and covariance matrix <math id="A6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}=(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{1}\mathbf{t}^{\top})^{\top}(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{1}\mathbf{t}^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}=(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{1}\mathbf{t}^{\top})^{\top}(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{1}\mathbf{t}^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math> for a point cloud <math id="A6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points. Suppose we obtain eigenvectors <math id="A6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{u}_{2},\mathbf{u}_{3}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{1},\mathbf{u}_{2},\mathbf{u}_{3}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct <math id="A6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> orthogonal matrices by concatenating them together. Depending on the collection of these matrices, we achieve equivariance to different motion groups: for <math id="A6.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(3)</annotation></semantics></math> equivariance, we use <math id="A6.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}=[\pm\mathbf{u}_{1},\pm\mathbf{u}_{2},\pm\mathbf{u}_{3}]\subset E(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}=[\pm\mathbf{u}_{1},\pm\mathbf{u}_{2},\pm\mathbf{u}_{3}]\subset E(3)</annotation></semantics></math>, and if we restrict this collection to contain only orthogonal, positive orientation matrices, we achieve <math id="A6.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SE(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SE(3)</annotation></semantics></math> equivariance. The frame then looks like,</p>
<table id="A6.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A6.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})=\{(\mathbf{U},\mathbf{t}):\mathbf{U}=[\pm\mathbf{u}_{1},\pm\mathbf{u}_{2},\pm\mathbf{u}_{3}])\}\subset E(3)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐭</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo>⊂</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x})=\{(\mathbf{U},\mathbf{t}):\mathbf{U}=[\pm\mathbf{u}_{1},\pm\mathbf{u}_{2},\pm\mathbf{u}_{3}])\}\subset E(3).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p2.4" class="ltx_p">For equivariant predictions on atomistic point clouds, we set <math id="A6.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{1}(g)\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{x}\mathbf{U}^{\top}+\mathbf{1}\mathbf{t}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱𝐔</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐭</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{1}(g)\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{x}\mathbf{U}^{\top}+\mathbf{1}\mathbf{t}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{2}(g)\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{x}\mathbf{U}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐱𝐔</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{2}(g)\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{x}\mathbf{U}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> for each frame element, followed by the application of the averaging operator in Eq. <a href="#A6.E28" title="In Appendix F Frame Averaging ‣ Appendix ‣ Platonic Transformers: A Solid Choice For Equivariance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>. Furthermore, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Puny et al. (2022)</a></cite> show that if <math id="A6.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>) is bounded, FA preserves the expressiveness of the underlying backbone, making <math id="A6.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\Phi\rangle_{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\Phi\rangle_{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> maximally expressive even for non-compact groups like <math id="A6.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Details of Architecture</h3>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide additional details about the architecture of the Platonic Transformer and the various model configurations used in our experiments. Our framework is designed to be equivariant to roto-translation groups, primarily <math id="A7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SE(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SE(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and, through specific configurations, the full Euclidean group <math id="A7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the core embedding dimension per group element as <math id="A7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{hidden}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>hidden</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{hidden}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since our features are functions on a group <math id="A7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="A7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{G}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{G}|</annotation></semantics></math>, the total feature dimension of a layer is <math id="A7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{model}}=|\mathcal{G}|\times d_{\text{hidden}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>hidden</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{model}}=|\mathcal{G}|\times d_{\text{hidden}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The specific group is determined by the <span id="A7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">solid_name</span> parameter.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the initial feature processing, input scalars and vectors are first embedded and then lifted into a group-equivariant feature space using an initial lifting operation. This creates a tensor where the channel dimension is expanded by a factor of <math id="A7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{G}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{G}|</annotation></semantics></math>. An initial group-equivariant linear layer then projects these lifted features to the model’s working dimension, <math id="A7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{model}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{model}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Optionally, an equivariant Absolute Positional Encoding (APE), parameterized by <span id="A7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ape_sigma</span>, can be added at this stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The main body of the network consists of a stack of equivariant transformer blocks. Each block contains two main sub-modules: a group-equivariant interaction layer and a feed-forward network (FFN), connected with residual connections. Normalization is applied either before each sub-module or after.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For the group-equivariant interaction layer, we denote the number of attention heads per group element as <math id="A7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{head}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>head</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{head}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The total number of effective parallel heads is therefore <math id="A7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{G}|\times n_{\text{head}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>head</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{G}|\times n_{\text{head}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The dimension of each head, <math id="A7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{head}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>head</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{head}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is calculated as <math id="A7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{hidden}}/n_{\text{head}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>hidden</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>head</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{hidden}}/n_{\text{head}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The input features are first projected to query, key, and value representations using group-equivariant linear layers. To encode relative spatial information, group-equivariant Rotary Position Embeddings, parameterized by <span id="A7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rope_sigma</span> and <span id="A7.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">learned_freqs</span>, are applied to the query and key vectors. The interaction can then be performed either as a full softmax-based attention mechanism or as a linear-time dynamic group convolution via the <span id="A7.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">attention</span> flag. For the Feed Forward Networks (FFNs), we denote the hidden feature dimension as <math id="A7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{ffn}}=d_{\text{model}}\times f_{\text{factor}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>ffn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>factor</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{ffn}}=d_{\text{model}}\times f_{\text{factor}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The FFN consists of two group-equivariant linear layers with a GELU activation function in between.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For the final output, two separate readout heads project the features to the desired scalar and vector output dimensions. For graph-level tasks, a pooling operation performs a mean aggregation over the node and group dimensions to produce a final invariant prediction. For node-level tasks, an averaging operation over the group axis projects the features back to standard invariant scalar and equivariant vector representations. Following standard Transformer practices, we apply dropout to the attention weights and FFN activations, and stochastic depth to the outputs of the equivariant transformer blocks.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Particular values for all the important hyperparameters used for the experiments are in the Table.<a href="#A11.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ K.3 Additional details on wall-clock timings ‣ Appendix K Details of experiments on QM9 ‣ Appendix ‣ Platonic Transformers: A Solid Choice For Equivariance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Hyperparameter Tuning and Model Selection Strategy</h3>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section outlines the full procedure used to configure and train our models.</p>
</div>
<section id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baseline Optimization</h5>

<div id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To establish a fair point of comparison, we first optimized the general training protocol using only the translation-only equivariant (<math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(n)</annotation></semantics></math>) models. This initial phase involved tuning the optimizer, learning rate schedule, weight decay, and data augmentations to ensure the baseline models were as competitive as possible. This fixed protocol was then used for all subsequent experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hyperparameter Sweep for Model Selection</h5>

<div id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the training protocol fixed, we performed an extensive hyperparameter sweep for both <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SE(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SE(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(n)</annotation></semantics></math> model classes. This sweep was designed to find the optimal architectural parameters while maintaining an equal computational budget between model families. The parameters and their swept values are summarized in Table <a href="#A8.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Hyperparameter Sweep for Model Selection ‣ Appendix H Hyperparameter Tuning and Model Selection Strategy ‣ Appendix ‣ Platonic Transformers: A Solid Choice For Equivariance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A8.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Hyperparameter Sweep Configurations.</figcaption>
<table id="A8.T6.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A8.T6.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T6.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A8.T6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter</span></td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A8.T6.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Configuration</span></td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A8.T6.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Values</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T6.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T6.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Hidden Dim</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">-</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="A8.T6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[384,576,768,1152]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>384,576,768</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1152</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[384,576,768,1152]</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T6.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T6.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" rowspan="4"><span id="A8.T6.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Number of Heads</span></td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="A8.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(n)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.T6.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> model</span> (HS=16)</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="A8.T6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[24,36,48,72]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>36</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>48</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>72</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[24,36,48,72]</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T6.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T6.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A8.T6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(n)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.T6.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> model</span> (HS=32)</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><math id="A8.T6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[12,18,24,36]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[12,18,24,36]</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T6.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T6.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A8.T6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SE(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SE(n)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.T6.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> model</span> (HS=16)</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><math id="A8.T6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2,3,4,6]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2,3,4,6]</annotation></semantics></math> heads per group element</td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T6.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T6.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="A8.T6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SE(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SE(n)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A8.T6.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> model</span> (HS=32)</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><math id="A8.T6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,2,3]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,2,3]</annotation></semantics></math> heads per group element</td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T6.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T6.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Rope Sigma (<math id="A8.T6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{rope}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>rope</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{rope}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">RoPE frequency scaling</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="A8.T6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.5-2.0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.5-2.0]</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T6.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T6.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Attention</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Full Attention / Linear Conv</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="A8.T6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\text{True},\text{False}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>True</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>False</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\text{True},\text{False}]</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T6.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T6.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Solid Group (<math id="A8.T6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>)</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Symmetry group</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="A8.T6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\text{Octahedron},\text{Tetrahedron},C_{2-8},D_{4-8}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mtext>Octahedron</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>Tetrahedron</mtext><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\text{Octahedron},\text{Tetrahedron},C_{2-8},D_{4-8}]</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T6.2.10" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T6.2.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Lambda F (<math id="A8.T6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>)</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">OMol Force loss weight</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="A8.T6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1.0-25.0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>25.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1.0-25.0]</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T6.2.11" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T6.2.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Batch Size</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Samples per batch</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="A8.T6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[64-512]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[64-512]</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A8.T6.2.12" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A8.T6.2.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">Weight Decay</td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"></td>
<td id="A8.T6.2.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><math id="A8.T6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1e^{-3}-1e^{-7}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1e^{-3}-1e^{-7}]</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The hyperparameter sweep was conducted across multiple layers and three random seeds for a <em id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">moderate number of epochs</em> to efficiently explore the configuration space. It should be noted that some configurations are not applicable for the Octahedral group, as its 24 symmetry elements require a minimum of 24 total effective heads (i.e., at least one head per group element). After identifying the best-performing hyperparameters for both the <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SE(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SE(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(n)</annotation></semantics></math> model families from this sweep, we proceeded to a final, full-length training run. These selected models were trained for a <em id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">large number of epochs</em> to ensure convergence again with fixed compute budget, producing the final results reported in the main paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A9" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix I </span>Details of experiments on Cifar10</h3>

<section id="A9.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">I.1 </span>Description of the dataset</h4>

<div id="A9.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The CIFAR-10 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krizhevsky, 2009</a>)</cite> is a standard benchmark for image classification, consisting of 60,000 32x32 color images across 10 classes. The dataset is divided into a training set of 50,000 images and a test set of 10,000 images.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A9.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">I.2 </span>Training Details</h4>

<div id="A9.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the CIFAR-10 classification task, our experimental setup is closely adapted from the supervised training recipe for Vision Transformers presented in DeiT-III <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. We tokenize each image into a sequence of non-overlapping patches using a patch size of <math id="A9.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 4</annotation></semantics></math> pixels, a key deviation from the ImageNet configurations to suit the lower resolution of the dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The model is trained using the LAMB optimizer, which is subject to a cosine decay schedule following a 5-epoch warm-up period. A comprehensive suite of regularization techniques is employed, including a weight decay of 0.02, Mixup with an alpha value of 0.8, and CutMix with an alpha of 1.0, in addition to model-size-dependent Stochastic Depth. The data augmentation pipeline is built upon the ‘3-Augment‘ strategy, incorporating standard Random Resized Crop (RRC), horizontal flips, ColorJitter with a factor of 0.3, and a single, randomly selected transformation from a pool of three: Grayscale, Solarization, or Gaussian Blur.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The training objective is optimized using a Binary Cross-Entropy (BCE) loss, and positional information is supplied to the transformer blocks through a combination of both Absolute Positional Encodings (APE) and Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE). Further hyperparameter details are available in Table <a href="#A11.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ K.3 Additional details on wall-clock timings ‣ Appendix K Details of experiments on QM9 ‣ Appendix ‣ Platonic Transformers: A Solid Choice For Equivariance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A10" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix J </span>Details of experiments on ScanObjectNN</h3>

<section id="A10.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">J.1 </span>Description of the dataset</h4>

<div id="A10.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A10.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ScanObjectNN<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Uy et al., 2019</a>)</cite> dataset is a real-world 3D point cloud dataset. It contains 15,000 objects divided into 15 categories with 2902 unique object instances. It contains background, parts missing, and object deformation elements, which makes the classification task a challenge. The dataset consists of three variants OBJ_BG, OBJ_ONLY and PB_T50_RS, for now the latter is only examined.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A10.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">J.2 </span>Training Details</h4>

<div id="A10.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A10.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to prepare the input point cloud <math id="A10.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> for processing by the Platonic Transformer, we follow a preprocessing procedure. Similar to established methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pang et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2022)</a></cite>, we first use Farthest Point Sampling (FPS) to select a set of L=2048 central points, denoted as <math id="A10.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{C}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A10.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=2048" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=2048</annotation></semantics></math>. Subsequently, for each central point <math id="A10.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{C}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{C}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a local patch <math id="A10.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{p}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{p}^{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> by identifying its K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) within the original point cloud P. These local patches serve as the primary input vectors to the Platonic Transformer.</p>
</div>
<div id="A10.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A10.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, to account for the axis-aligned nature of the dataset and to provide the model with a global reference frame, we incorporate rotation augmentation. For each input vector, a rotation matrix is applied. This matrix is either a random rotation or the 3×3 identity matrix, which is concatenated with the input vector to provide the model with information about the global orientation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A11" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix K </span>Details of experiments on QM9</h3>

<section id="A11.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">K.1 </span>Description of the dataset</h4>

<div id="A11.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A11.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The QM9 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramakrishnan et al., 2014</a>)</cite> contains up to 9 heavy atoms and 29 atoms, including hydrogens. We use the train/val/test partitions introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gilmer et al. (2017)</a></cite>, which consist of 100K/18K/13K samples, respectively, for each partition.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A11.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">K.2 </span>Training Details for the Regression Experiment</h4>

<div id="A11.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A11.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the QM9 regression task, we train the Platonic Transformer to predict molecular properties. Before being fed to the model, the input molecular geometries are centered by subtracting the mean coordinate of each molecule. To stabilize training, we normalize the target property values by subtracting their mean and dividing by their standard deviation, with these statistics computed over the training set. We employ data augmentation in the form of random <math id="A11.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math> rotations applied to the coordinates during training.</p>
</div>
<div id="A11.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A11.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The model is trained for a total of 1000 epochs using a batch size of 96. We utilize the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of <math id="A11.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and a weight decay of <math id="A11.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>. A cosine annealing schedule with a 10-epoch linear warmup adjusts the learning rate throughout training. To prevent exploding gradients, we apply gradient clipping with a maximum norm of 0.5. The training objective is the Mean Absolute Error (MAE) on the normalized target values, while validation and testing are performed by calculating the MAE on the original, unnormalized scale. Our experiments explore different Platonic Transformer configurations, specifically by varying the symmetry group among <span id="A11.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">trivial_3</span>, <span id="A11.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tetrahedron</span>, and <span id="A11.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">octahedron</span>. Further hyperparameter details are available in Table <a href="#A11.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ K.3 Additional details on wall-clock timings ‣ Appendix K Details of experiments on QM9 ‣ Appendix ‣ Platonic Transformers: A Solid Choice For Equivariance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A11.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A11.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We choose FAFormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to compare the contribution of Frame Averaging (with a standard Transformer backbone) with our end-to-end trained Platonic Transformer with different platonic solid symmetry groups. Recent efforts in long-context sequence modeling have also pit Transformer-based methods with state-space methods. To provide a similar analysis here, we choose G-Hyena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moskalev et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, a recent state-space model that relies on long convolutions for 3D molecular point clouds. We hyperparameter-tune FAFormer<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              We use the open-source implementation of FAFormer provided at <a href="https://github.com/Graph-and-Geometric-Learning/Frame-Averaging-Transformer" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/Graph-and-Geometric-Learning/Frame-Averaging-Transformer</a>. We obtain the source code for G-Hyena through private correspondence with the authors.</span></span></span> and G-Hyena to have similar parameter counts and representational capacity as our best-performing Platonic Transformer on QM9. The baselines are also trained over 500 epochs with a batch size of 96 on a single H200 GPU.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A11.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">K.3 </span>Additional details on wall-clock timings</h4>

<div id="A11.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A11.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To produce the wall-clock timing for the standard Transformer in Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ QM9 ‣ 5.3 Equivariant Tasks ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Platonic Transformers: A Solid Choice For Equivariance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, node features from <math id="A11.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=64</annotation></semantics></math> QM9 molecules were projected from <math id="A11.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{in}}=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{in}}=11</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A11.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{model}}=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{model}}=512</annotation></semantics></math> using a Linear layer and fed as tokens into a <span id="A11.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TransformerEncoderLayer</span> module provided by PyTorch with 16 heads. We measure wall-clock timings for a forward pass over 10 batches on a single H200 GPU. This offers a reference timing to compare the inference speed of the Platonic Transformer and other geometric baselines like G-Hyena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moskalev et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and the Tensor Field Network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thomas et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A11.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Hyperparameter for all datasets</figcaption>
<table id="A11.T7.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A11.T7.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A11.T7.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A11.T7.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameter</span></td>
<td id="A11.T7.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A11.T7.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QM9</span></td>
<td id="A11.T7.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A11.T7.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OMol25</span></td>
